<section id="article-terminology">
<div class="article-reader">
<a href="#articles" class="back-btn">← Back to articles</a>
<div class="meta-bar"><span class="tag">Beginner</span><span>5 min read</span><span>August 2026</span></div>
<h1>Cryptocurrency Terminology</h1>
<p>In this article, we provide you with a crypto dictionary containing some of the slang, terminology and native language used in the cryptocurrency space that every crypto novice should know.</p>
<div class="term-grid">
<div class="term-card"><h4>Node</h4><p>A computer or device that participates in a blockchain network, storing data, verifying transactions, and maintaining network security.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Altcoin</h4><p>Short for "alternative coin." Any cryptocurrency other than Bitcoin. Ethereum, Solana, and Cardano are examples.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>HODL</h4><p>Originated from a 2013 typo of "hold." Now means "Hold On for Dear Life" — keeping crypto long-term despite price swings.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Weak Hands</h4><p>Investors likely to sell when prices fall or volatility rises. Slang for those who struggle to stay invested during uncertainty.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Blockchain</h4><p>A distributed digital ledger that records transactions across a network of computers, grouped into linked blocks.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>FOMO</h4><p>Fear Of Missing Out. The anxiety of seeing a crypto price rise rapidly and feeling pressured to buy before it's "too late."</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>FOBO</h4><p>Fear Of a Better Option. Hesitation to commit because you worry a better opportunity might appear later.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>FUD</h4><p>Fear, Uncertainty, and Doubt. Negative info that creates fear around a crypto project. Not all criticism is FUD — do your research.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Shitcoin</h4><p>Informal slang for a cryptocurrency considered to have little value, weak fundamentals, or poor utility. Subjective and often used as an insult.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Dead Coin</h4><p>A crypto project that has effectively stopped operating or been abandoned. Consider development, community, and trading activity.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Zombie Coin</h4><p>A project that appears largely inactive or abandoned but still has some market activity. Volume can be misleading on small exchanges.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Fiat Money</h4><p>Government-issued currency not backed by a physical commodity like gold. Examples: South African rand (ZAR), US dollar (USD).</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>DeFi</h4><p>Decentralized Finance — financial apps built on blockchains that operate without traditional banks. Includes lending, borrowing, and trading via smart contracts.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>DeFi Degen</h4><p>Slang for someone who takes highly speculative approaches in DeFi, chasing high yields without fully researching risks. From "degenerate."</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Rug Pull</h4><p>A scam where project creators drain funds or remove liquidity, leaving investors with losses. Always research before investing.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><h4>Pump and Dump</h4><p>Market manipulation where promoters hype a low-liquidity token to inflate its price, then sell off — leaving later buyers with losses.</p></div>
</div>
<p>These are just a few of the terminologies used in the crypto space. As you continue learning, you will come across many more terms relating to blockchain technology, trading, DeFi, NFTs, wallets, and crypto security.</p>
<div class="video-box"><p>Prefer visual learning? <a href="https://youtu.be/XNMHvlLjTI4" target="_blank">Watch this beginner-friendly video</a> explaining common cryptocurrency terms and slang.</p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="article-nfts">
<div class="article-reader">
<a href="#articles" class="back-btn">← Back to articles</a>
<div class="meta-bar"><span class="tag">NFTs</span><span>8 min read</span><span>August 2026</span></div>
<h1>NFTs: What Are NFTs? — Things You Need to Know About Listing Your NFTs</h1>
<p>There are many different marketplaces and platforms in the NFT space, including OpenSea, Zora and others. It is important to understand that not every platform works in exactly the same way.</p>
<h2>What Are NFTs?</h2>
<p>NFT stands for <strong>Non-Fungible Token</strong>. An NFT is a unique digital asset recorded on a blockchain. NFTs can represent digital artwork, music, videos, collectibles and other types of digital content. NFTs are not limited to pictures. Creators can use NFTs for different forms of digital art, including music, videos and other creative work.</p>
<h2>Understanding NFT Fees</h2>
<p>One of the first things you need to understand when entering the NFT space is that creating an account on a marketplace and carrying out blockchain transactions are not necessarily the same thing.</p>
<p>Blockchain transactions can involve what are known as <strong>gas fees</strong>. Gas fees are payments made to blockchain validators or network participants for processing certain transactions. Gas fees are not paid to OpenSea, and OpenSea does not control blockchain gas prices. The amount can vary depending on the blockchain and network conditions.</p>
<p>Therefore, it would be inaccurate to say that NFT gas fees always cost between $50 and $300 or that there is always a single one-time fee that allows you to list unlimited NFTs. Your actual costs depend on the specific blockchain transactions you make.</p>
<p>OpenSea also has its own marketplace fees. As of May 2026, OpenSea states that its typical fee for selling NFTs is <strong>1%</strong>. Fees can change, so it is always wise to check OpenSea's current fee information before making a transaction.</p>
<h2>Creator Earnings and Royalties</h2>
<p>Creator earnings allow NFT creators to receive a portion of the sale price when their NFTs are sold again. However, you should not assume that setting a percentage automatically guarantees that you will receive that percentage from every secondary sale. OpenSea explains that creator earnings can be enforced or optional, depending on how the collection is configured. When earnings are optional, the NFT owner may have the ability to adjust whether the creator receives the suggested amount. This is an important change from older NFT guides that presented royalties as guaranteed payments.</p>
<h2>Listing Your NFTs</h2>
<div class="step-card"><div class="step-number">1</div><h4>Create a Crypto Wallet</h4><p>A crypto wallet allows you to manage blockchain assets and interact with decentralized applications. MetaMask is one popular option. When setting up, you will receive a <strong>Secret Recovery Phrase (SRP)</strong> — typically 12 words. Never share this with anyone. Keep it secure and offline. If you lose it, MetaMask cannot recover it for you.</p></div>
<div class="step-card"><div class="step-number">2</div><h4>Connect Your Wallet to OpenSea</h4><p>Access OpenSea through a web browser and connect your wallet. Pay close attention to what you are approving. Never enter your Secret Recovery Phrase into a website simply because it claims you need it to connect.</p></div>
<div class="step-card"><div class="step-number">3</div><h4>Create Your NFT</h4><p>OpenSea provides NFT creation tools through OpenSea Studio. You can create a collection and mint NFTs without coding knowledge. You will need a collection logo, name, token symbol, blockchain choice, NFT media, name, description, and optional traits. Deploying a smart contract requires a blockchain transaction and may require gas fees.</p></div>
<div class="step-card"><div class="step-number">4</div><h4>Upload Your NFT</h4><p>Upload your NFT's media and add information such as name, description, and supply. If you create a unique NFT with a supply of one, that item will have a single edition. OpenSea currently uses ERC-1155 for NFTs created through its Create Collection process.</p></div>
<div class="step-card"><div class="step-number">5</div><h4>Set Your Creator Earnings</h4><p>Configure the percentage you would like to receive from eligible secondary sales. Remember: creator earnings can be enforced or optional on OpenSea. Do not assume every secondary sale will automatically pay royalties.</p></div>
<div class="step-card"><div class="step-number">6</div><h4>List Your NFT for Sale</h4><p>Choose a fixed-price sale or another available selling format. Before confirming, carefully review the details in your wallet, including network, transaction, and any applicable fees. Gas fees can change — never rely on old articles for fixed amounts.</p></div>
<h2>Marketing Your NFTs</h2>
<p>Creating and listing an NFT is only one part of the process. Marketing is essential if you want people to discover your work. Simply uploading an NFT and waiting for someone to buy it is unlikely to be enough. You need to introduce your work to potential collectors and build an audience. Promote through social media, communities, content, and other legitimate marketing methods. The NFT space is highly competitive — good artwork is only part of the journey.</p>
<blockquote>Most importantly, always protect your wallet and your Secret Recovery Phrase. Losing access to your wallet or giving your recovery phrase to a scammer can put your assets at risk.</blockquote>
<div class="video-box"><p>Visual learner? Check out these videos: <a href="https://youtu.be/NNQLJcJEzv0" target="_blank">NFT Guide Part 1</a> and <a href="https://youtu.be/Oz9zw7-_vhM" target="_blank">NFT Guide Part 2</a></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="article-ethereum">
<div class="article-reader">
<a href="#articles" class="back-btn">← Back to articles</a>
<div class="meta-bar"><span class="tag">Ecosystem</span><span>7 min read</span><span>August 2026</span></div>
<h1>Let's Talk About the Ethereum Ecosystem</h1>
<p>According to different cryptocurrency market-data sources, Ether (ETH), which is the native cryptocurrency of the Ethereum blockchain, is one of the largest and most widely traded crypto assets in the market. However, saying that ETH is the "highest-performing" cryptocurrency can be misleading because performance depends on the period and measurement being used. Crypto prices can change dramatically over short periods of time, so it is important to always look at current data and do your own research before making financial decisions.</p>
<p>Ethereum is one of the most important blockchain networks in the cryptocurrency space. It is a decentralized blockchain and software platform that allows developers to build and deploy smart contracts, decentralized applications (dApps), tokens and other blockchain-based applications.</p>
<p>The idea for Ethereum was proposed by <strong>Vitalik Buterin</strong> in late 2013. Several other people later became co-founders, including Gavin Wood, Joseph Lubin, Anthony Di Iorio, Charles Hoskinson, Mihai Alisie, Amir Chetrit and Jeffrey Wilcke. Ethereum's development continued through 2014, and the Ethereum mainnet officially launched on <strong>July 30, 2015</strong>.</p>
<p>The purpose of Ethereum was to create a blockchain that could do more than simply transfer digital money. Ethereum was designed as a platform where developers could build applications that operate using blockchain technology and smart contracts.</p>
<h2>What Are Smart Contracts?</h2>
<p>One of the most important features of Ethereum is smart contracts. A smart contract is a program stored on a blockchain that automatically executes instructions when the conditions written into the program are met. Smart contracts can allow people to interact with applications without relying on a traditional intermediary to manually execute every step.</p>
<p>However, it is important to understand that smart contracts are not simply digital versions of traditional legal contracts. They are computer programs, and their behaviour depends on the code that has been written and deployed.</p>
<p>For example, imagine a decentralized lending application where a person deposits cryptocurrency as collateral and borrows another digital asset. The smart contract can contain rules determining how much collateral is required, how the loan works, and what happens if the value of the collateral falls below a particular threshold. If the conditions defined by the protocol are met, the smart contract can automatically perform the programmed action — including liquidating collateral when required.</p>
<p>This is one of the major ideas behind decentralized finance: rules can be enforced by blockchain-based software rather than relying entirely on a traditional financial institution. However, this does not mean that smart contracts can never fail or that there is "no room for foul play." Smart contracts can contain bugs, vulnerabilities or poorly designed rules.</p>
<p>Ethereum's transactions and smart-contract operations also require <strong>gas</strong>. Gas measures the computational work required to execute operations on Ethereum, and users pay fees for that computation.</p>
<h2>Ethereum vs. Bitcoin</h2>
<p>Bitcoin came before Ethereum and was launched as a decentralized digital currency and payment network. Ethereum took a different approach by creating a general-purpose programmable blockchain.</p>
<p>This does not mean that Bitcoin has no programmable functionality. Bitcoin uses a scripting system that can support different types of transaction conditions. The important difference is that Ethereum was specifically designed around a more general-purpose environment for running smart-contract programs and decentralized applications. This made it possible for developers to build entire ecosystems of applications on top of Ethereum.</p>
<p>Ethereum has also continued to evolve through network upgrades. For example, the <strong>Dencun upgrade</strong> introduced data blobs designed to make it cheaper for Layer 2 networks to store certain data on Ethereum. The <strong>Pectra upgrade</strong> followed in May 2025 with changes affecting Ethereum's execution and consensus layers.</p>
<p>Another major change in Ethereum's history was its transition from proof-of-work mining to <strong>proof-of-stake in September 2022</strong>. Today, Ethereum is secured by validators who stake ETH and participate in processing and securing the network.</p>
<h2>What Makes the Ethereum Ecosystem Different?</h2>
<h3>1. Decentralized Applications (dApps)</h3>
<p>Ethereum provides an environment where developers can build decentralized applications. These applications can use smart contracts to perform different functions without relying entirely on a centralized company to control the underlying application. Ethereum has become home to applications covering DeFi, NFTs, gaming, stablecoins, decentralized social applications and more.</p>
<h3>2. Smart Contracts</h3>
<p>Smart contracts allow developers to create programmable rules that are executed by the Ethereum network. This is one of the biggest reasons Ethereum became more than just a cryptocurrency. Developers can build applications where transactions and other actions are controlled by code.</p>
<h3>3. Tokens</h3>
<p>Ethereum made it possible for developers to create different types of tokens using standards such as <strong>ERC-20</strong> and <strong>ERC-721</strong>. This has helped create a huge ecosystem of cryptocurrencies, stablecoins, NFTs and other digital assets that operate using Ethereum's infrastructure.</p>
<h3>4. Layer 2 Networks</h3>
<p>The Ethereum ecosystem is no longer limited to transactions taking place directly on Ethereum's main network (Layer 1). Layer 2 networks are built to process transactions using Ethereum as part of their security and settlement infrastructure. These networks are an important part of Ethereum's scaling strategy. Recent upgrades have specifically helped reduce the cost of data used by rollups, making Layer 2 networks more efficient.</p>
<h3>5. Continuous Development</h3>
<p>Ethereum is continuously being upgraded. Unlike the idea that Ethereum simply launched in 2015 and has remained unchanged, the network has gone through numerous upgrades over the years. These upgrades have changed how Ethereum operates and have introduced improvements for users, developers and validators.</p>
<h2>Examples of Projects in the Ethereum Ecosystem</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Golem</strong> — A decentralized computing network designed to allow users to share and access computing resources.</li>
<li><strong>Uniswap</strong> — A decentralized cryptocurrency exchange that allows users to trade digital assets through smart contracts rather than using a traditional centralized exchange.</li>
<li><strong>Chainlink</strong> — A decentralized oracle network designed to connect blockchain-based smart contracts with data and information from outside blockchain networks.</li>
<li><strong>Aragon</strong> — A project focused on tools for creating and managing decentralized organizations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Ethereum vs. Ether (ETH)</h2>
<p>This is where many cryptocurrency beginners become confused. <strong>Ethereum</strong> refers to the blockchain network and its broader ecosystem. <strong>Ether (ETH)</strong> is the native cryptocurrency of the Ethereum network.</p>
<p>ETH is used for several purposes within the Ethereum ecosystem, including paying transaction and computation fees. ETH can also be staked by validators as part of Ethereum's proof-of-stake security system.</p>
<p>A lot of people in the cryptocurrency space use the words "Ethereum" and "ETH" interchangeably during casual conversations. Technically, however, they are not the same thing. Think of it this way: <strong>Ethereum = the network and platform. ETH = the native cryptocurrency of that network.</strong></p>
<h2>The Ethereum Ecosystem Today</h2>
<p>Today, Ethereum supports a broad ecosystem involving smart contracts, decentralized finance, NFTs, stablecoins, wallets, Layer 2 networks, oracles, decentralized applications, developers and validators. Ethereum is still evolving, and new applications and infrastructure continue to be built around it.</p>
<p>That is one of the most interesting things about Ethereum: it is not simply a cryptocurrency. It is a blockchain platform that provides infrastructure on which other applications and digital assets can be built.</p>
